I added `app/javascript/tags/ColorContrastWarning.jsx`, but I haven't run it. This checkout has no `node_modules`, so Jest wouldn't start. I removed the temporary test I'd written for the check.

```jsx
export const ColorContrastWarning = ({ textColor, backgroundColor }) => {
  const ratio = WCAGColorContrast.ratio(
    textColor.replace('#', ''),
    backgroundColor.replace('#', ''),
  );

  if (ratio >= MIN_CONTRAST_RATIO) return null;

  return <p role="alert">These colors are hard to read together</p>;
};
```

- **Contrast math:** it reuses the repo's existing WCAG helper (`app/javascript/utilities/color/WCAGColorContrast.js`) instead of a new calculation. That helper expects colors without the `#`, so the component strips it first. It has its own tests.
- **Output:** the warning is a plain `<p role="alert">`, so screen readers announce it when it appears. There's no styling beyond that.
- **Bad input will crash:** the helper throws if a color isn't valid hex. With the six-digit hex you described that never happens. But if the props come from a half-typed color input, the component would throw while rendering. I can add a guard for that if you want it.
- **No translations:** the message is plain English, like the strings in the other component in `app/javascript/tags/`. `AGENTS.md` asks for all locales to be updated, but I didn't find any translation setup in that folder, so I didn't add any.

To check it, run `yarn install`, then a quick Jest test with a light grey such as `#777777` on white (should warn) and `#1a2b3c` on white (should render nothing). I can add that as a permanent test file if you'd like.
